  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains in place over Utah this\nevening, though slightly flattened due to a trough crossing the\nIdaho Panhandle and heading into Montana. The high pressure will\nrebound over Utah over the weekend, with greatest heights on\nSunday. Patchy high clouds will continue crossing the region \nthrough Saturday, otherwise dry and stable conditions can be \nexpected under this pattern. Temperatures will very gradually\ntrend warmer through Sunday.\n\nOn Monday, the ridge axis will shift east as a weakening trough\nmoves into the Great Basin. This trough is expected to cross Utah\nMonday night into Tuesday. Expect generally little impact from\nthis trough. Latest models continue to trend lower on QPF. The NBM\n75th percentile has mostly 0 QPF across Utah with this trough, \nwith less than 0.05 inches over the Uintas. Temperatures should \ndrop slightly by Tuesday. High pressure is then progged to build \nback into the area for the second half of next week. \n\nSouthwest flow may increase next weekend ahead of a potentially\ndeepening trough. However, while the majority of global ensembles\ndepict some sort of trough moving into Utah late next weekend or\nearly next week, cluster analysis of global ensembles currently \nshow appreciable model spread in terms of the strength and timing \nof any such trough.",
